    "news_text":"Extensive searches for the Bramble Cay melomys, a small rat-like animal, have failed to find a single specimen from its only known habitat on a sandy island in far northern Australia Climate change appears to have driven to extinction an Australian Great Barrier Reef rodent, according to a new study, which suggests the species may be the first mammal lost to the global phenomenon. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Extensive searches for the Bramble Cay melomys, a small rat-like animal, have failed to find a single specimen from its only known habitat on a sandy island in far northern Australia. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Researchers said the key factor behind the extinction was \"almost certainly\" ocean inundation of the low-lying cay, likely on several occasions, over the last decade which resulted in dramatic habitat loss. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR Available data on sea-level rise and weather events in the Torres Strait region \"point to human-induced climate change being the root cause of the loss of the Bramble Cay melomys\", added the Queensland state government and University of Queensland study. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR The Melomys rubicola, considered the Great Barrier Reef's only endemic (found nowhere else) mammal species, was first discovered on the cay in 1845 by Europeans who shot the \"large rats\" for sport. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR But the last known sighting, by a professional fisherman, was in 2009. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR When a 2014 study found no sign of the species, researchers decided to conduct the most extensive survey possible in the hope of conserving the species. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR That survey\u2014using traps, cameras and searches\u2014\"produced no records of the species, confirming that the only known population of this rodent is now extinct\", said the report. NEWLINE_CHAR NEWLINE_CHAR The 2015 IUCN Red List of Threatened Species implicated climate change in the extinction of another mammal, the Little Swan Island 